      prompt replies.  They all suggested similar approaches, i.e. include
      the conditions in the subscript.  Simple, why didn't I think about
      it, it seems I have been doing this thousand of times????  Here it is
      anyway, original message following.

ttt.test <- inc.all[, c('formule', 'encincf', 'en15incf', 'unsousi')]

ttt.match2 <- ttt.test[, 'formule'] == '2'
ttt.match5 <- ttt.test[, 'formule'] == '5'
ttt.test[ttt.match2, c('encincf', 'en15incf', 'unsousi')] <-
  ttt.test[ttt.match2, c('encincf', 'en15incf', 'unsousi')] * 1.10
ttt.test[ttt.match5, c('encincf', 'en15incf', 'unsousi')] <-
  ttt.test[ttt.match5, c('encincf', 'en15incf', 'unsousi')] * 0.90

and this ran pretty fast for such a large data set.

