From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Nov  2 16:18:23 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Fri, 02 Nov 2001 16:18:23 +0000
Subject: Re: Questions on Correlations for hierarchical data and
In-Reply-To: <3BE2A591.3D060265@virginia.edu>
References: <3BE2A591.3D060265@virginia.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.WNT.4.31.0111021614340.628-100000@gannet>

On Fri, 2 Nov 2001, Frank E Harrell Jr wrote:

> To my knowledge the construct is.na(x) <- logical value
> does not make sense.

It's standard S4, implemented in S-PLUS 6.  It means for each element of x,
set to missing if the corresponding element of the rhs (after recycling) is
true.  Mostly (but not always)

is.na(x) <- l

is equivalent to

x[l] <- NA,

strings (the class) being one exception.  Green Book pp. 199-200.

